    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_059aa25c743849297e147a916d0050ac2f304dec1632e7089d7a056d04a0f4e1",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source links its smoothing levels in a concrete chain. Mellin contour displacement expresses the exponentially smoothed von Mangoldt discrepancy through separately classified residues, and Bernoulli subtraction removes the regular and trivial-zero contribution. Under RH, zero-counting and tail control then let a low conjugate pair force two-sided oscillation at the smoothing scale. Riesz averaging produces an absolutely convergent zero series and, later, the cumulative bound needed to make the correction term lower order when passing from the logarithmically weighted count through the prime-power count to pi(x)-Li(x). Smoothing therefore determines both where zero phases can be controlled and whether sign-sensitive information survives return to a sharper counting representation.",
