    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_904afb1d7352c2d8d4cde10e4bf12a82a56dabe1cd7aa91f55c5d65d678f75bb",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Section 3 builds a reusable localization calculus for oscillatory contour integrals, and Section 4 repurposes that calculus to obtain short-interval critical-line zero information. The first stage reduces power-weighted zero sums to cumulative control, converts the hard unweighted case to a von Mangoldt Dirichlet series, discards controlled contour pieces, and isolates the only coherent stationary range with a finite error ledger. The later stage defines a real critical-line transform, bounds its signed integral by splitting the associated Dirichlet-series phases around at most one stationary frequency, and observes that constant sign would remove cancellation. A second contour evaluation then supplies a main term proportional to the interval length, contradicting the small signed-integral bound and forcing an odd-order zero.",
