    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_9d774209da0329d34309f8e16bf1300f4ebc23cae29a892ca38739bfedcce772",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source exhibits two distinct, non-interchangeable routes from zero information to arithmetic sign phenomena. For the Dirichlet-L prime race, a completed product bounds the aggregate zero contribution, a prime-power split isolates the prime layer, and positive integral kernels preserve the resulting one-sided lower bound while inserting power and logarithmic convergence factors. For the zeta prime-counting error, truncation makes simultaneous phase recurrence finite-dimensional, a separate tail estimate restores the full sine series, and a modified Lindelof argument transports interior growth to a boundary limit that is not uniformly continuous. Read together, the sections show why a zero expansion alone does not give an arithmetic sign conclusion: one route preserves a bias through coefficient reweighting, while the other creates and transfers two-sided oscillation through phase recurrence and boundary control.",
